ERR1 = "Avoid the abort statement"
ERR2 = "Mark this variable pragma Atomic before assigning to it from a task or an abortable part"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_6.3.3",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.3": "Published Standards/Ada 95/6.3.3 The Abort Statement",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.3": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Concurrency",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.3": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Avoid using the abort statement.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider using the asynchronous select statement rather than
the abort statement.</p>
<p>&#8226; Minimize uses of the asynchronous select statement.</p>
<p>&#8226; Avoid assigning nonatomic global objects from a task or from
the abortable part of an asynchronous select statement.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>If required in the application, provide a task entry for orderly
shutdown.</p>
<p>The following example of asynchronous transfer of control shows a
database transaction. The database operation may be cancelled (through
a special input key) unless the commit transaction has begun. The code
is extracted from the Rationale (1995, &sect;9.4):</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">with Ada.Finalization;
package Txn_Pkg is
   type Txn_Status is (Incomplete, Failed, Succeeded);
   type Transaction is new Ada.Finalization.Limited_Controlled with private;
   procedure Finalize (Txn : in out transaction);
   procedure Set_Status (Txn    : in out Transaction;
                         Status : in     Txn_Status);
private
   type Transaction is new Ada.Finalization.Limited_Controlled with
      record
         Status : Txn_Status := Incomplete;
         pragma Atomic (Status);
         . . . -- More components
      end record;
end Txn_Pkg;
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
package body Txn_Pkg is
   procedure Finalize (Txn : in out Transaction) is
   begin
      -- Finalization runs with abort and ATC deferred
      if Txn.Status = Succeeded then
         Commit (Txn);
      else
         Rollback (Txn);
      end if;
   end Finalize;
   . . . -- body of procedure Set_Status
end Txn_Pkg;
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- sample code block showing how Txn_Pkg could be used:
declare
   Database_Txn : Transaction;
   -- declare a transaction, will commit or abort during finalization
begin
   select  -- wait for a cancel key from the input device
      Input_Device.Wait_For_Cancel;
      -- the Status remains Incomplete, so that the transaction will not commit
   then abort  -- do the transaction
      begin
         Read (Database_Txn, . . .);
         Write (Database_Txn, . . .);
         . . .
         Set_Status (Database_Txn, Succeeded);
         -- set status to ensure the transaction is committed
      exception
         when others =>
            Ada.Text_IO.Put_Line ("Operation failed with unhandled exception:");
            Set_Status (Database_Txn, Failed);
      end;
   end select;
   -- Finalize on Database_Txn will be called here and, based on the recorded
   -- status, will either commit or abort the transaction.
end;
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>When an abort statement is executed, there is no way to know what the
targeted task was doing beforehand. Data for which the target task is
responsible might be left in an inconsistent state. The overall effect
on the system of aborting a task in such an uncontrolled way requires
careful analysis. The system design must ensure that all tasks
depending on the aborted task can detect the termination and respond
appropriately.</p>
<p>Tasks are not aborted until they reach an abort completion point such
as beginning or end of elaboration, a delay statement, an accept
statement, an entry call, a select statement, task allocation, or the
execution of an exception handler. Consequently, the abort statement
might not release processor resources as soon as you might expect. It
also might not stop a runaway task because the task might be executing
an infinite loop containing no abort completion points. There is no
guarantee that a task will not abort until an abort completion point in
multiprocessor systems, but the task will almost always stop running
right away.</p>
<p>An asynchronous select statement allows an external event to cause a
task to begin execution at a new point, without having to abort and
restart the task (Rationale 1995, &sect;9.3). Because the triggering
statement and the abortable statement execute in parallel until one of
them completes and forces the other to be abandoned, you need only one
thread of control. The asynchronous select statement improves
maintainability because the abortable statements are clearly delimited
and the transfer cannot be mistakenly redirected.</p>
<p>In task bodies and in the abortable part of an asynchronous select,
you should avoid assigning to nonatomic global objects, primarily
because of the risk of an abort occurring before the nonatomic
assignment completes. If you have one or more abort statements in your
application and the assignment is disrupted, the target object can
become abnormal, and subsequent uses of the object lead to erroneous
execution (Ada Reference Manual, &sect;9.8). In the case of scalar
objects, you can use the attribute 'Valid, but there is no equivalent
attribute for nonscalar objects. (See Guideline 5.9.1 for a discussion
of the 'Valid attribute.) You also can still safely assign to local
objects and call operations of global protected objects.</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>The first bullet is automated: a bare <code>abort</code> statement is a
distinct lexer signature from the <code>then abort</code> keyword pair that marks
an asynchronous select's abortable part (disambiguated by checking
whether the token immediately before <code>then</code> is a <code>;</code>, which only
happens for the async-select form &#8212; an <code>if</code>/<code>elsif</code> condition
never ends a statement with a semicolon before its own <code>then</code>). Every
occurrence is flagged. The second bullet ("consider an asynchronous
select instead") is not a separate check &#8212; it is simply the recommended
fix for whatever the first bullet flags, so it has no violation shape of
its own.</p>
<p>The fourth bullet is automated for the direct case: a package-level
variable (excluding constants, and excluding one marked <code>pragma
Atomic</code>) is flagged at each <code>:=</code> assignment that falls within a task
body or within the abortable part of an asynchronous select &#8212; found the
same way <code>ADA95_6.2.4</code> finds cross-file usage, via
<code>ent.refs("Setby")</code> directly from the variable's own entity, so no
project-wide scan is needed. <code>Setby</code> also fires when the variable is
passed as an out or in-out parameter to a call, which is not what the
guideline means by "assigning" (the callee owns its own atomicity, not
the caller), so each occurrence is confirmed by checking that a
<code>:=</code> immediately follows it before being flagged. This does not follow
assignments to a record component or an
array element (only a directly named object), and it does not attempt
to prove an abort statement actually exists anywhere in the program to
make the risk real &#8212; the guideline itself says to avoid the pattern
regardless.</p>
<p>"Minimize uses of the asynchronous select statement" is not automated:
it is the same bare "minimize a count, no threshold given" pattern
already found non-automatable for <code>ADA95_6.2.1</code> and
<code>ADA95_6.2.6</code>.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


_OPENER_KEYWORDS = ("if", "case", "loop", "select", "begin", "do")


def _next_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.next()

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.next()

    return lex


def _previous_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.previous(True, True)

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.previous(True, True)

    return lex


def _is_statement_start(lex):
    prev = _previous_significant(lex)

    return prev is None or prev.text() in (";", "then", "else", "loop", "begin", "do")


def _is_async_select_then(lex):
    # An async select's "then" follows the triggering statement's own
    # ";" - unlike an if/elsif's "then", which always follows a boolean
    # expression and can never be preceded by a bare ";".
    prev = _previous_significant(lex)

    return prev is not None and prev.text() == ";"


def _find_abort_statements(lexer):
    results = []
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "abort"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            prev = _previous_significant(lex)

            if not (prev and prev.text().lower() == "then" and _is_async_select_then(prev)):
                results.append((lex.line_begin(), lex.column_begin()))

        lex = lex.next()

    return results


def _skip_balanced(lex):
    # Advance past one fully nested if/case/loop/select/begin/do
    # construct, landing just after its own matching "end" (and that
    # end's completer keyword, if any).
    depth = 1
    lex = _next_significant(lex)

    while lex and depth > 0:
        text = lex.text().lower() if lex.token() == "Keyword" else None

        if text in _OPENER_KEYWORDS:
            depth += 1
        elif text == "end":
            depth -= 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)

            if (
                lex
                and lex.token() == "Keyword"
                and lex.text().lower() in ("if", "case", "loop", "select")
            ):
                lex = _next_significant(lex)

            continue

        lex = _next_significant(lex)

    return lex


def _find_abortable_part_ranges(lexer):
    # For each "select ... then abort ... end select;" in the file,
    # returns the (start_line, end_line) of the abortable part (from
    # "abort" up to, but not including, the terminating "end select").
    ranges = []
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "select"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            depth = 0
            inner = _next_significant(lex)
            abortable_start = None

            while inner:
                text = inner.text().lower() if inner.token() == "Keyword" else None

                if text in _OPENER_KEYWORDS:
                    depth += 1
                    inner = _next_significant(inner)
                    continue

                if text == "end":
                    if depth == 0:
                        if abortable_start is not None:
                            ranges.append((abortable_start, inner.line_begin()))
                        break

                    depth -= 1
                    inner = _next_significant(inner)

                    if (
                        inner
                        and inner.token() == "Keyword"
                        and inner.text().lower() in ("if", "case", "loop", "select")
                    ):
                        inner = _next_significant(inner)

                    continue

                if (
                    depth == 0
                    and abortable_start is None
                    and text == "then"
                    and _is_async_select_then(inner)
                ):
                    abort_kw = _next_significant(inner)

                    if abort_kw and abort_kw.text().lower() == "abort":
                        abortable_start = abort_kw.line_begin()
                        inner = _next_significant(abort_kw)
                        continue

                inner = _next_significant(inner)

        lex = lex.next()

    return ranges


def _package_level_variables(file):
    variables = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare", "Object ~Constant ~Protected ~Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        parent = ent.parent()

        if parent is not None and parent.kind().check("Package"):
            variables.append((ent, decl_ref))

    return variables


def _atomic_ids(lexer):
    ids = set()
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "pragma":
            name_lex = _next_significant(lex)

            if name_lex and name_lex.text().lower() == "atomic":
                paren = _next_significant(name_lex)

                if paren and paren.text() == "(":
                    arg_lex = _next_significant(paren)
                    ent = arg_lex.ent() if arg_lex else None

                    if ent:
                        ids.add(ent.id())

        lex = lex.next()

    return ids


def _task_ranges(file):
    ranges = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare Body", "Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        end_ref = ent.ref("Endby Body")

        if not end_ref:
            continue

        ranges.append((decl_ref.line(), end_ref.line()))

    return ranges


def _in_any_range(line, ranges):
    return any(start <= line <= end for start, end in ranges)


def _check_abort_statements(check, file):
    lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)

    for line, col in _find_abort_statements(lexer):
        check.violation(None, file, line, col, ERR1)


def _is_assignment_target(lexer, ref):
    # "Setby" also fires for an out/in-out parameter passed to a call
    # (the callee is responsible for its own atomicity, not the caller),
    # which is not what the guideline means by "assigning" - only a
    # ":=" immediately after this occurrence is a genuine assignment.
    lex = lexer.lexeme(ref.line(), ref.column())
    lex = _next_significant(lex) if lex else None

    return lex is not None and lex.text() == ":="


def _check_nonatomic_assignments(check, file):
    atomic_ids = _atomic_ids(file.lexer(lookup_ents=True))

    for ent, decl_ref in _package_level_variables(file):
        if ent.id() in atomic_ids:
            continue

        for set_ref in ent.refs("Setby ~Init"):
            set_file = set_ref.file()
            lexer = set_file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)

            if not _is_assignment_target(lexer, set_ref):
                continue

            task_ranges = _task_ranges(set_file)
            abortable_ranges = _find_abortable_part_ranges(lexer)

            if _in_any_range(set_ref.line(), task_ranges) or _in_any_range(
                set_ref.line(), abortable_ranges
            ):
                check.violation(ent, set_file, set_ref.line(), set_ref.column(), ERR2)


def check(check, file):
    _check_abort_statements(check, file)
    _check_nonatomic_assignments(check, file)
